      <h2>5. How Browser-Based 1v1 Video Chat Works</h2>

      <p>Many browser-based video-chat services use WebRTC, a set of web technologies designed for real-time communication between browsers and devices.</p>

      <h3>5.1 WebRTC</h3>

      <p>WebRTC provides browser APIs for real-time audio, video and data communication. It can allow browsers to establish a direct connection when network conditions permit.</p>

      <h3>5.2 Signaling</h3>

      <p>Before a connection can be established, the participants' browsers need to exchange information required to negotiate the session. This process is commonly called signaling.</p>

      <h3>5.3 ICE, STUN and TURN</h3>

      <p>Direct connections are not always possible because users may be behind routers, firewalls or network address translation. WebRTC uses ICE to find a workable network path.</p>

      <p>STUN can help a device discover a network-facing address. When a direct connection cannot be established, a TURN server can relay traffic between participants.</p>

      <h3>5.4 Adaptive Video Quality</h3>

      <p>Network quality can change while a call is in progress. A video system may adjust resolution, bitrate or other media parameters to keep the call usable.</p>

      <p>This means a video call may change quality during a session rather than maintaining one fixed resolution from beginning to end.</p>

