      <h2>7. Responsible Anonymity: Staying Safe in 1v1 Chat</h2>

      <p>Anonymity can make it easier to start a conversation, but it also means that you should not automatically trust information provided by another participant.</p>

      <h3>7.1 Do Not Share Sensitive Information</h3>

      <p>Avoid sharing passwords, one-time passwords, financial information, government identification numbers, home addresses or other sensitive information.</p>

      <h3>7.2 Be Careful With Links</h3>

      <p>Do not open suspicious links sent by strangers. A link can lead to phishing pages, malicious downloads or other unwanted content.</p>

      <h3>7.3 Remember That Video Reveals More Than You Think</h3>

      <p>Your camera can reveal your surroundings. Check the background before starting a video conversation and avoid displaying documents, screens or objects containing personal information.</p>

      <h3>7.4 Leave Uncomfortable Conversations</h3>

      <p>You do not need to continue a conversation that makes you uncomfortable. Use the available leave, skip, block or report controls when appropriate.</p>

      <h2>8. What Could Change in 1v1 Chat?</h2>

      <p>The future of one-to-one online communication is likely to involve improvements in matching, moderation, accessibility and connection quality. However, the exact direction will depend on user needs, technology and regulation.</p>

      <h3>8.1 Better Matching</h3>

      <p>Platforms may use optional preferences such as language, interests or conversation topics to make random matching more relevant.</p>

      <h3>8.2 More Accessible Communication</h3>

      <p>Improvements in captions, audio processing and browser compatibility could make real-time conversations easier for a wider range of users.</p>

      <h3>8.3 More Sophisticated Moderation</h3>

      <p>Automated systems may become better at identifying potentially harmful content, while platforms continue to balance moderation with privacy and false-positive concerns.</p>

      <h3>8.4 Greater Privacy Transparency</h3>

      <p>As users become more aware of online data practices, clear explanations of collection, retention and third-party processing can become increasingly important when choosing a communication service.</p>

